  "name": "Question 10",
  "problem": "At each point $P$ on a particular curve, the derivative ${y}^{\u2032}(x)$ is **inversely** proportional to the square of the slope of the line through $P$ and the origin. The derivative at the point $(-2,3)$ equals $4.$ What differential equation describes this curve?",
  "worked_solution": "Let's highlight the important words in the given statement:\n\n*At each point $P$ on a particular curve, the **derivative** ${y}^{\u2032}(x)$ is **inversely proportional** to the **square of the slope of the line through $P$ and the origin**.*\n\nWe note the following:\n\n- The *slope of the line through $P(x,y)$ and the origin* is given by $\\frac{y}{x}.$\n\n- Since the *derivative* is *inversely proportional to* the square of the slope of the line through $P$ and the origin, we have \n\n$$\n{y}^{\u2032}(x)=k\\cdot {[{(\\frac{y}{x})}^{2}]}^{-1}=k\\cdot {(\\frac{x}{y})}^{2}\n$$\n\nwhere $k$ is a constant.\n\nSo, our differential equation is\n\n$$\n{y}^{\u2032}(x)=\\frac{k{x}^{2}}{{y}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nIn addition, we're told that ${y}^{\u2032}(x)=4$ when at the point $(-2,3).$ We can use this to determine the value of the coefficient $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4=\\frac{k(-2)^{2}}{{3}^{2}}\\,\u27f9\\,k=9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the differential equation describing the curve is\n\n$$\n{y}^{\u2032}(x)=\\frac{9{x}^{2}}{{y}^{2}}.\n$$",
